WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.199 --> 00:00:01.173
- Good morning Believe Nation,

00:00:01.173 --> 00:00:02.628
my name is Evan Carmichael.

00:00:02.628 --> 00:00:04.658
My one word is believe and I believe

00:00:04.658 --> 00:00:05.925
that entrepreneurs will solve

00:00:05.925 --> 00:00:08.365
all the major problems of the world.

00:00:08.365 --> 00:00:11.462
Today's message is don't quit.

00:00:11.462 --> 00:00:13.425
Over to you, Colonel Sanders.

00:00:13.425 --> 00:00:15.847
(rooster crowing)

00:00:15.847 --> 00:00:18.416
♫ I wake up every morning

00:00:18.416 --> 00:00:21.270
♫ Entspresso keep me going

00:00:21.270 --> 00:00:25.276
♫ I wake up every morning

00:00:25.276 --> 00:00:27.091
- I was working for a living,

00:00:27.091 --> 00:00:30.168
I've raised a restaurant
for about 25 years.

00:00:30.168 --> 00:00:31.336
Trying to serve the best food

00:00:31.336 --> 00:00:33.408
that I had served to the public.

00:00:33.408 --> 00:00:35.458
That's where I developed
my chicken recipe,

00:00:35.458 --> 00:00:37.370
that I wanted to have the
best chicken in my restaurant

00:00:37.370 --> 00:00:38.669
than any competitor has.

00:00:38.669 --> 00:00:41.736
So I was on the highway
where they go to Florida,

00:00:41.736 --> 00:00:46.272
the Smoky Mountains, and
as they went down to there,

00:00:46.272 --> 00:00:48.774
I wanted to get them as they come back,

00:00:48.774 --> 00:00:51.267
and you'd find out, surprising
how long they'd forgot

00:00:51.267 --> 00:00:52.651
all about the price of the food

00:00:52.651 --> 00:00:54.133
before they forgot the quality,

00:00:54.133 --> 00:00:57.028
come back again for that good food.

00:00:57.028 --> 00:00:58.560
And I was recently with Duncan Hines,

00:00:58.560 --> 00:00:59.790
his Adventures in Good Eating,

00:00:59.790 --> 00:01:02.616
and that gave me a very
select clientele too.

00:01:02.616 --> 00:01:05.217
I had a moral obligation to
give people something good,

00:01:05.217 --> 00:01:07.566
something worthwhile.

00:01:07.566 --> 00:01:08.598
And I loved it.

00:01:08.598 --> 00:01:11.655
Then they come along, surveyed
this interstate system,

00:01:11.655 --> 00:01:12.947
went seven miles back of me,

00:01:12.947 --> 00:01:15.970
left me sitting high and dry.

00:01:15.970 --> 00:01:17.361
I was 66 years old.

00:01:17.361 --> 00:01:19.587
I couldn't afford to quit.

00:01:19.587 --> 00:01:21.886
Course when they were talking about it,

00:01:21.886 --> 00:01:23.717
for three of four years before,

00:01:23.717 --> 00:01:25.244
looked like it was going to be a reality,

00:01:25.244 --> 00:01:27.223
I begun to put out a guinea pig franchise

00:01:27.223 --> 00:01:29.186
because I was thinking what can I do next?

00:01:29.186 --> 00:01:31.922
They going to wipe out this restaurant.

00:01:31.922 --> 00:01:33.871
And I thought, well I tried
to franchise the chicken,

00:01:33.871 --> 00:01:38.293
so I gained four or five of
these guinea pig franchises.

00:01:38.293 --> 00:01:40.225
I proved to myself that I had a project

00:01:40.225 --> 00:01:41.564
that would take with the public,

00:01:41.564 --> 00:01:45.098
profitable to the restaurant man,

00:01:45.098 --> 00:01:47.065
and also it was a go, just Merriam Rider

00:01:47.065 --> 00:01:51.234
getting behind it and
getting it put over for good.

00:01:51.234 --> 00:01:53.203
So when they did survey the system,

00:01:53.203 --> 00:01:54.576
I was left sitting high and dry

00:01:54.576 --> 00:01:56.326
so that wiped me out.

00:01:57.804 --> 00:01:59.363
- Isn't that something?

00:01:59.363 --> 00:02:03.995
Here's a case where disaster
really was a blessing.

00:02:03.995 --> 00:02:06.344
Something that could have destroyed you,

00:02:06.344 --> 00:02:08.204
God turned it around really.

00:02:08.204 --> 00:02:09.789
I think sometimes people think

00:02:09.789 --> 00:02:12.351
that instead of laying down and dying

00:02:12.351 --> 00:02:14.584
when disaster comes, you can use it

00:02:14.584 --> 00:02:16.182
as stepping stones into something greater.

00:02:16.182 --> 00:02:17.486
- Into stepping stones for something else.

00:02:17.486 --> 00:02:18.931
There's no question about that.

00:02:18.931 --> 00:02:21.734
So my question was what could
I save out of that wreck?

00:02:21.734 --> 00:02:23.647
You want to be wrecked?

00:02:23.647 --> 00:02:24.786
And the chicken was taken,

00:02:24.786 --> 00:02:28.250
proven it had taken with the public.

00:02:28.250 --> 00:02:33.083
And all I had to do was stay
with it and get it goin.

00:02:33.083 --> 00:02:36.059
I think the moral to my
life is, don't quit at 65.

00:02:36.059 --> 00:02:37.998
Maybe your boat hasn't come in yet.

00:02:37.998 --> 00:02:40.038
(host and audience laughs)

00:02:40.038 --> 00:02:41.601
- So I loved the story of Colonel Sanders,

00:02:41.601 --> 00:02:43.604
he's one of my favorite
stories and examples

00:02:43.604 --> 00:02:45.649
to give of an older entrepreneur.

00:02:45.649 --> 00:02:47.719
This guy didn't really start franchising

00:02:47.719 --> 00:02:50.392
his business, KFC, until he was in his 60s

00:02:50.392 --> 00:02:51.802
and didn't really become an icon

00:02:51.802 --> 00:02:55.043
or someone super well-known
until he was in his 70s.

00:02:55.043 --> 00:02:58.429
How many people quit too
soon on their business?

00:02:58.429 --> 00:03:00.130
I can't tell you how
many people come up to me

00:03:00.130 --> 00:03:02.939
and say Evan, I'm 25 years old,

00:03:02.939 --> 00:03:05.936
is it too late to get started?

00:03:05.936 --> 00:03:08.075
What are you talking about?

00:03:08.075 --> 00:03:10.611
And really just comes down
to just your belief system.

00:03:10.611 --> 00:03:12.703
Here was a guy who was 62 years old

00:03:12.703 --> 00:03:15.005
when he was franchising
KFC, where a lot of people

00:03:15.005 --> 00:03:17.666
would be saying I'm
getting ready to retire,

00:03:17.666 --> 00:03:19.022
I'm getting ready to slow down,

00:03:19.022 --> 00:03:21.064
I'm getting ready to wind things up,

00:03:21.064 --> 00:03:23.174
just ease into the rest of my life,

00:03:23.174 --> 00:03:26.101
and here's a guy who said,
no, I'm going to crush it.

00:03:26.101 --> 00:03:27.274
I'm just getting started.

00:03:27.274 --> 00:03:29.707
I want to build an empire.

00:03:29.707 --> 00:03:31.758
And this really comes down
to whether you believe

00:03:31.758 --> 00:03:34.258
it can happen or it can't happen,

00:03:34.258 --> 00:03:35.425
you're right.

00:03:35.425 --> 00:03:37.504
If you believe at 62 years old

00:03:37.504 --> 00:03:40.194
that you're too old to get
started on something new,

00:03:40.194 --> 00:03:41.027
then you're right.

00:03:41.027 --> 00:03:42.434
And you won't take action.

00:03:42.434 --> 00:03:44.084
But if you believe that
age is just a number

00:03:44.084 --> 00:03:46.908
and 62 means you've got lots of experience

00:03:46.908 --> 00:03:49.069
and you're ready to go
crush some new project,

00:03:49.069 --> 00:03:50.308
then you're right as well.

00:03:50.308 --> 00:03:52.267
And so you have to be really careful

00:03:52.267 --> 00:03:54.952
to pay attention to the story
that you're telling yourself

00:03:54.952 --> 00:03:56.930
because a lot of times,
we're holding ourselves back

00:03:56.930 --> 00:03:59.233
with these excuses of we're too old,

00:03:59.233 --> 00:04:00.477
we're too young,

00:04:00.477 --> 00:04:01.929
we've come from the wrong country,

00:04:01.929 --> 00:04:03.306
we don't have the right education,

00:04:03.306 --> 00:04:04.375
you don't have the right parents,

00:04:04.375 --> 00:04:05.508
on and on and on,

00:04:05.508 --> 00:04:07.477
and as long as you believe
that reinforcing story,

00:04:07.477 --> 00:04:10.148
then it's going to continue to perpetuate.

00:04:10.148 --> 00:04:12.568
Now, in terms of don't quitting,

00:04:12.568 --> 00:04:13.549
there's a lot of advice saying,

00:04:13.549 --> 00:04:15.816
don't quit, don't quit
don't quit, don't quit.

00:04:15.816 --> 00:04:18.301
You should not quit when it's your dream.

00:04:18.301 --> 00:04:19.134
When this is something that you

00:04:19.134 --> 00:04:21.439
really, really, really want to do,

00:04:21.439 --> 00:04:22.911
then you can't quit on it.

00:04:22.911 --> 00:04:25.205
But if it's something that
you've lost the passion for,

00:04:25.205 --> 00:04:27.045
you don't feel energized by it anymore,

00:04:27.045 --> 00:04:29.225
it feels like work to show up,

00:04:29.225 --> 00:04:30.668
then that's when you quit.

00:04:30.668 --> 00:04:33.088
And so when people ask me,
should I quit this thing or not?

00:04:33.088 --> 00:04:34.119
Is it still your dream?

00:04:34.119 --> 00:04:35.266
Are you still passionate about it?

00:04:35.266 --> 00:04:36.536
Are you still excited about it?

00:04:36.536 --> 00:04:38.841
'Cause if you are, then
you enjoy the process.

00:04:38.841 --> 00:04:40.274
And yes you want to get the results,

00:04:40.274 --> 00:04:41.296
and yes you want to win,

00:04:41.296 --> 00:04:42.449
and yes you want to make the money,

00:04:42.449 --> 00:04:43.932
and yes you want to have that impact,

00:04:43.932 --> 00:04:45.828
but you still enjoy the process.

00:04:45.828 --> 00:04:47.492
I like making YouTube videos.

00:04:47.492 --> 00:04:48.692
This is fun for me.

00:04:48.692 --> 00:04:49.992
It's enjoyable for me.

00:04:49.992 --> 00:04:52.135
I'm growing through it,
and even if I wasn't

00:04:52.135 --> 00:04:54.570
on the path to hitting
a million subscribers,

00:04:54.570 --> 00:04:57.601
I would still be doing it
because I love doing it.

00:04:57.601 --> 00:04:59.755
And if I was on the path to
hitting a million subscribers

00:04:59.755 --> 00:05:01.316
and I stopped liking it,

00:05:01.316 --> 00:05:02.347
then I would leave,

00:05:02.347 --> 00:05:03.180
I would sell it,

00:05:03.180 --> 00:05:05.517
I would go do something
else because you cannot do

00:05:05.517 --> 00:05:08.324
great things when you
don't love what you do.

00:05:08.324 --> 00:05:09.845
So as long as it's still your passion,

00:05:09.845 --> 00:05:11.252
as long as it's still your dream,

00:05:11.252 --> 00:05:13.212
you have to keep going or
you're going to live the rest

00:05:13.212 --> 00:05:14.778
of your life in regret.

00:05:14.778 --> 00:05:15.965
But if you've lost that passion,

00:05:15.965 --> 00:05:16.798
then get out, immediately,

00:05:16.798 --> 00:05:19.614
and go do something
else that does bring you

00:05:19.614 --> 00:05:22.624
excitement, joy, energy, and fulfillment.

00:05:22.624 --> 00:05:24.467
So the question today is, I'm curious,

00:05:24.467 --> 00:05:26.470
what is the story that
you have historically

00:05:26.470 --> 00:05:29.382
told yourself as to why
you can't be successful,

00:05:29.382 --> 00:05:31.030
like I'm too old,

00:05:31.030 --> 00:05:33.721
what is the story that you
have consistently told yourself

00:05:33.721 --> 00:05:34.667
in the past?

00:05:34.667 --> 00:05:36.087
We're going to shed it now.

00:05:36.087 --> 00:05:37.758
It's gone after today.

00:05:37.758 --> 00:05:39.777
What has that story been?

00:05:39.777 --> 00:05:41.081
Leave it in the comments below,

00:05:41.081 --> 00:05:42.328
super curious to find out.

00:05:42.328 --> 00:05:44.746
I also want to give a quick
shout out to John Milleker Jr.,

00:05:44.746 --> 00:05:46.445
John thank you so much
for picking up a copy

00:05:46.445 --> 00:05:48.079
of my book, Your One Word.

00:05:48.079 --> 00:05:49.980
It really, really,
really means a lot to me,

00:05:49.980 --> 00:05:51.664
and I hope you're enjoying the read.

00:05:51.664 --> 00:05:53.040
Thank you guys again for watching.

00:05:53.040 --> 00:05:54.283
I believe in you.

00:05:54.283 --> 00:05:56.290
I hope you continue to believe in yourself

00:05:56.290 --> 00:05:58.211
and whatever your one word is,

00:05:58.211 --> 00:06:00.723
much love and I'll see
you again tomorrow morning

00:06:00.723 --> 00:06:03.811
for another shot of entspresso.

00:06:03.811 --> 00:06:06.474
♫ I wake up every morning

00:06:06.474 --> 00:06:10.057
♫ Entspresso keep me going

00:06:12.531 --> 00:06:13.757
- See unfortunately a lot of people stand

00:06:13.757 --> 00:06:15.005
in front of the stove of life,

00:06:15.005 --> 00:06:16.679
and they say now stove,
you give me some meat,

00:06:16.679 --> 00:06:18.410
then I'll put some wood in you.

00:06:18.410 --> 00:06:19.532
That ain't the way it works.

00:06:19.532 --> 00:06:23.329
You got to put something in
before you can get anything out.

00:06:23.329 --> 00:06:25.437
So many times the employee goes

00:06:25.437 --> 00:06:27.159
to the employer and say, give me a raise,

00:06:27.159 --> 00:06:29.158
then I'll start coming to work on time.

00:06:29.158 --> 00:06:32.239
Or so many times they
will come to me and say,

00:06:32.239 --> 00:06:33.525
make me the boss.

00:06:33.525 --> 00:06:34.883
Now I know I haven't been here very long,

00:06:34.883 --> 00:06:36.262
don't really deserve to be the boss,

00:06:36.262 --> 00:06:39.857
but I just function better
when I am in charge of things.

00:06:39.857 --> 00:06:41.449
You reward me now, and
then I'll promise ya,

00:06:41.449 --> 00:06:44.511
I'll learn what this business
is all about later on.

00:06:44.511 --> 00:06:47.231
Reward me now, and I'll produce later.

00:06:47.231 --> 00:06:48.461
It doesn't work that way.

00:06:48.461 --> 00:06:50.541
Can't you just see a
youngster in school saying,

00:06:50.541 --> 00:06:52.736
teacher, if I take a failing grade home,

00:06:52.736 --> 00:06:54.737
my parents are going to skin me alive.

00:06:54.737 --> 00:06:56.659
Pass me on this quarter and next time,

00:06:56.659 --> 00:06:58.570
I'll study more than anybody else.

00:06:58.570 --> 00:07:00.923
Reward me now, I'll produce later.

00:07:00.923 --> 00:07:02.045
It doesn't work that way.

00:07:02.045 --> 00:07:03.938
Can't you just see an old farmer

00:07:03.938 --> 00:07:05.825
standing out in the fields
in October and saying,

00:07:05.825 --> 00:07:07.717
Lord I know I didn't
plant a thing this year,

00:07:07.717 --> 00:07:09.517
but if you give me a big crop this year,

00:07:09.517 --> 00:07:12.663
I'll plant more than anybody next year.

00:07:12.663 --> 00:07:14.203
It ain't that way, folks.

00:07:14.203 --> 00:07:17.515
You got to put something
in before you can expect

00:07:17.515 --> 00:07:18.964
to get anything out.

00:07:18.964 --> 00:07:20.430
Well, he's just a pumpin' away,

00:07:20.430 --> 00:07:21.346
you know, that's hard.

00:07:21.346 --> 00:07:22.179
It's all good.

00:07:22.179 --> 00:07:24.061
So I mean, the question is,

00:07:24.061 --> 00:07:26.199
just how much pumpin' are you going to do

00:07:26.199 --> 00:07:27.701
before I drink a water?

00:07:27.701 --> 00:07:29.188
And finally old Bernard
said, you know Jimmy,

00:07:29.188 --> 00:07:30.568
I don't believe there's
any water down there.

00:07:30.568 --> 00:07:32.200
Jimmy said, yeah it is Bernard.

00:07:32.200 --> 00:07:34.523
You know in south Alabama,
the wells are deep.

00:07:34.523 --> 00:07:36.742
We're glad they're deep
because the deeper the well,

00:07:36.742 --> 00:07:38.085
the cooler, the cleaner, the sweeter,

00:07:38.085 --> 00:07:40.394
the purer, the better tasting the water.

00:07:40.394 --> 00:07:42.300
And isn't that true in life?

00:07:42.300 --> 00:07:44.263
Isn't it true that if you can become an MD

00:07:44.263 --> 00:07:46.379
by six weeks in summer
school, that the rewards

00:07:46.379 --> 00:07:48.922
would be almost minimal or nothing?

00:07:48.922 --> 00:07:51.280
And how many patients would you have?

00:07:51.280 --> 00:07:53.787
Isn't it true that if
you become a sales expert

00:07:53.787 --> 00:07:56.071
in three days of a training school,

00:07:56.071 --> 00:07:59.254
that the rate of pay would
go down rather radically?

00:07:59.254 --> 00:08:02.235
Isn't it true that anything worth doing

00:08:02.235 --> 00:08:04.068
is worth doing poorly,

00:08:05.538 --> 00:08:08.371
until you can learn to do it well?

00:08:09.642 --> 00:08:11.549
We'll never know how many kids have missed

00:08:11.549 --> 00:08:13.627
a college scholarship
because they didn't study

00:08:13.627 --> 00:08:15.857
an average of 10 more minutes a day?

00:08:15.857 --> 00:08:19.458
We will never know how we
come so close to promotion,

00:08:19.458 --> 00:08:22.161
but we grew discouraged and quit too soon.

00:08:22.161 --> 00:08:24.667
We'll never know how much more success

00:08:24.667 --> 00:08:27.009
we would have had had we just had

00:08:27.009 --> 00:08:29.116
a little more pumping in there,

00:08:29.116 --> 00:08:31.426
and pump and pump and pump and pump.

00:08:31.426 --> 00:08:33.945
Well finally, old Bernard
just got disgusted,

00:08:33.945 --> 00:08:34.779
threw up his hands and said,

00:08:34.779 --> 00:08:36.157
Jimmy, there's just no water down there.

00:08:36.157 --> 00:08:38.460
Jimmy said, no don't
stop Bernard, don't stop.

00:08:38.460 --> 00:08:41.366
If you stop, the water's going
to go all the way back down,

00:08:41.366 --> 00:08:45.500
and then you're going to
have to start all over.

00:08:45.500 --> 00:08:48.324
The reality is folks, and I'm
totally convinced of this,

00:08:48.324 --> 00:08:49.686
this is the story of America,

00:08:49.686 --> 00:08:50.818
this is your story.

00:08:50.818 --> 00:08:52.179
This is the story of success.

00:08:52.179 --> 00:08:53.620
This is the story of life.

00:08:53.620 --> 00:08:55.135
I believe with all of my heart

00:08:55.135 --> 00:08:56.937
that if you will pump long enough,

00:08:56.937 --> 00:08:59.536
and hard enough, and
enthusiastically enough,

00:08:59.536 --> 00:09:01.259
that eventually the reward is,

00:09:01.259 --> 00:09:02.961
you're going to follow the effort,

00:09:02.961 --> 00:09:06.584
and then once that water starts to flow,

00:09:06.584 --> 00:09:10.678
all you got to do is
just keep a little easy,

00:09:10.678 --> 00:09:12.313
steady pressure on it,

00:09:12.313 --> 00:09:13.553
and you're going to get more water

00:09:13.553 --> 00:09:16.081
than you can possibly use.

00:09:16.081 --> 00:09:17.645
The basic problem is this,

00:09:17.645 --> 00:09:21.092
so many times people get
involved in something,

00:09:21.092 --> 00:09:23.074
and they'll say, well I'll give it a try

00:09:23.074 --> 00:09:25.305
and if it works out, that'll be good.

00:09:25.305 --> 00:09:28.449
And if it doesn't work out, hey
I ain't going to kill a second,

00:09:28.449 --> 00:09:30.300
you know what I mean, feller?

00:09:30.300 --> 00:09:32.039
Well, I got to tell you something folks.

00:09:32.039 --> 00:09:35.034
You going to pump forever like
that before anything happens.

00:09:35.034 --> 00:09:36.192
When you get into something,

00:09:36.192 --> 00:09:38.354
grab that sucker and get with it.

00:09:38.354 --> 00:09:41.710
And then once the water starts to flow,

00:09:41.710 --> 00:09:43.164
then ladies and gentlemen,

00:09:43.164 --> 00:09:45.696
that's what Strategies
for Success is all about.

00:09:45.696 --> 00:09:46.775
- Now, here's an important point,

00:09:46.775 --> 00:09:49.056
nothing works the first time.

00:09:49.056 --> 00:09:51.468
When you try something new
it probably won't work.

00:09:51.468 --> 00:09:52.893
When you try something new several times,

00:09:52.893 --> 00:09:53.961
it probably won't work.

00:09:53.961 --> 00:09:55.596
And the turning point in my life came

00:09:55.596 --> 00:09:57.772
when I would hear good
ideas, and I was so eager

00:09:57.772 --> 00:09:58.947
to be successful in selling.

00:09:58.947 --> 00:10:00.622
I would run out and try the ideas,

00:10:00.622 --> 00:10:01.493
and they wouldn't work.

00:10:01.493 --> 00:10:03.031
I'd try a way of getting an appointment,

00:10:03.031 --> 00:10:05.021
or answering objection, or closing a sale,

00:10:05.021 --> 00:10:05.970
it wouldn't work.

00:10:05.970 --> 00:10:07.331
And my natural response,

00:10:07.331 --> 00:10:08.933
(groans)

00:10:08.933 --> 00:10:10.498
and I think, just be disappointed,

00:10:10.498 --> 00:10:12.929
and then I realized nothing works,

00:10:12.929 --> 00:10:14.526
at least the first few times.

00:10:14.526 --> 00:10:16.395
So I decided I would try a new idea,

00:10:16.395 --> 00:10:18.888
five or ten times before
I passed judgment on it.

00:10:18.888 --> 00:10:20.276
I would not just try it once and quit

00:10:20.276 --> 00:10:22.981
like most people do, and
that changed my whole life.

00:10:22.981 --> 00:10:24.147
It was a turning point in my life

00:10:24.147 --> 00:10:25.594
because I realized from then on,

00:10:25.594 --> 00:10:27.715
if you've got a good idea,
and you've got a good goal,

00:10:27.715 --> 00:10:28.910
and you want to double your income

00:10:28.910 --> 00:10:30.329
and improve the quality of your life,

00:10:30.329 --> 00:10:31.646
and you have to try new things

00:10:31.646 --> 00:10:33.220
in order to get new results,

00:10:33.220 --> 00:10:34.549
it's not going to work the first time.

00:10:34.549 --> 00:10:35.803
So you say well that didn't work,

00:10:35.803 --> 00:10:37.232
try something else and try something else,

00:10:37.232 --> 00:10:38.291
and try something else.

00:10:38.291 --> 00:10:40.425
Now if you try, only
two things can happen.

00:10:40.425 --> 00:10:41.780
What are they?

00:10:41.780 --> 00:10:42.879
(whispering from audience)

00:10:42.879 --> 00:10:43.712
Succeed or fail.

00:10:43.712 --> 00:10:45.121
If you succeed, you do more of it,

00:10:45.121 --> 00:10:47.575
if you fail, you learn from it,

00:10:47.575 --> 00:10:49.182
get smarter, and try it again.

00:10:49.182 --> 00:10:51.202
So you cannot lose by taking action.

00:10:51.202 --> 00:10:53.150
You can only lose by not taking action.

00:10:53.150 --> 00:10:55.370
- But why did you still
believe in the company?

00:10:55.370 --> 00:10:58.339
I have to think that at any point

00:10:58.339 --> 00:11:00.285
in Silicon Valley history,
there's a lot of people

00:11:00.285 --> 00:11:02.359
that at that point, they're just like,

00:11:02.359 --> 00:11:05.385
this isn't going to
work, market's changed.

00:11:05.385 --> 00:11:08.382
This isn't a viable concept anymore.

00:11:08.382 --> 00:11:11.965
- Well, you know, (laughs)

00:11:14.106 --> 00:11:17.416
I don't know how much belief I had,

00:11:17.416 --> 00:11:20.331
but I knew that I had
kind of gotten everybody

00:11:20.331 --> 00:11:23.331
into the boat with me,
so it wasn't so much

00:11:23.331 --> 00:11:25.337
that I didn't want to quit,

00:11:25.337 --> 00:11:27.616
I wanted to quit every
day I was in that job.

00:11:27.616 --> 00:11:30.030
I felt like throwing up when
I woke up in the morning.

00:11:30.030 --> 00:11:31.818
(audience laughs)

00:11:31.818 --> 00:11:33.015
- So you know what it's
like having a baby now?

00:11:33.015 --> 00:11:34.395
- Yeah, exactly.

00:11:34.395 --> 00:11:36.683
Like I felt like I was pregnant.

00:11:36.683 --> 00:11:37.811
(audience laughs)

00:11:37.811 --> 00:11:40.534
But you know, I'd hired every employee,

00:11:40.534 --> 00:11:41.639
and they all trusted me.

00:11:41.639 --> 00:11:42.730
I'd raised all the money.

00:11:42.730 --> 00:11:46.110
I talked to every investor
into putting their money in.

00:11:46.110 --> 00:11:49.546
I had given stock to my
friends and relatives,

00:11:49.546 --> 00:11:51.215
and they were all excited
about the prospects

00:11:51.215 --> 00:11:52.048
for the company.

00:11:52.048 --> 00:11:54.329
When it went public, a lot of my friends

00:11:54.329 --> 00:11:55.945
from high school bought the stock

00:11:55.945 --> 00:11:58.634
'cause I was running the company.

00:11:58.634 --> 00:12:02.801
And so I just felt like I
could die, but I couldn't quit.

00:12:03.988 --> 00:12:07.294
And one of the horrible things
about being founder, CEO

00:12:07.294 --> 00:12:10.172
is it's the one job you really can't quit.

00:12:10.172 --> 00:12:12.213
Or if you do quit, you're a punk.

00:12:12.213 --> 00:12:14.052
(audience laughs)

00:12:14.052 --> 00:12:16.385
So I didn't want to be a punk,

00:12:17.332 --> 00:12:20.415
so I just kept, I just kept peddling.

00:12:21.884 --> 00:12:23.658
But it was rough,

00:12:23.658 --> 00:12:25.101
running Loudcloud,

00:12:25.101 --> 00:12:27.382
and it didn't stop there, right?

00:12:27.382 --> 00:12:30.110
That was Loudcloud.
- Yeah.

00:12:30.110 --> 00:12:31.727
- Loudcloud,

00:12:31.727 --> 00:12:34.488
by the time Loudcloud was
two and a half years old,

00:12:34.488 --> 00:12:38.610
I had sold the Loudcloud
part of the business,

00:12:38.610 --> 00:12:40.718
and I had to change it
into another business,

00:12:40.718 --> 00:12:42.634
so the next thing that happened

00:12:42.634 --> 00:12:44.593
was Loud Cloud was going to go bankrupt,

00:12:44.593 --> 00:12:48.417
so I raised $160 million in the IPO.

00:12:48.417 --> 00:12:51.287
But we burnt through that,
and we couldn't stop the burn

00:12:51.287 --> 00:12:55.447
because the customers kept
going out of business.

00:12:55.447 --> 00:12:57.129
And so we were headed towards bankruptcy,

00:12:57.129 --> 00:12:58.546
so then I had to,

00:12:59.786 --> 00:13:02.659
I sold (laughs) the services
part of the business,

00:13:02.659 --> 00:13:04.572
and all the cash poured into EDS,

00:13:04.572 --> 00:13:07.110
and then I hit the layoff,

00:13:07.110 --> 00:13:09.317
probably another 1/3 of the employees,

00:13:09.317 --> 00:13:12.310
and then I kept the
remaining 80 employees,

00:13:12.310 --> 00:13:14.320
and we became a software company,

00:13:14.320 --> 00:13:16.217
a publicly traded software company

00:13:16.217 --> 00:13:19.114
with no customers and no revenue.

00:13:19.114 --> 00:13:20.928
- That was when you became Opsware?

00:13:20.928 --> 00:13:22.926
- That's when we became Opsware, yeah.

00:13:22.926 --> 00:13:25.042
So that was hard.

00:13:25.042 --> 00:13:26.812
(host laughs)

00:13:26.812 --> 00:13:27.733
But it was good, you know.

00:13:27.733 --> 00:13:29.928
That kind of stuff, it makes you strong.

00:13:29.928 --> 00:13:31.468
(host laughs)

00:13:31.468 --> 00:13:33.956
Eventually, five years later, we sold

00:13:33.956 --> 00:13:36.956
to Hewlett-Packard for $1.6 billion.

00:13:38.011 --> 00:13:39.125
- Not a bad exit.

00:13:39.125 --> 00:13:42.970
- Which was like, I'm still
shocked by that whole thing.

00:13:42.970 --> 00:13:44.439
I still wake up in the
middle of the night going,

00:13:44.439 --> 00:13:46.534
are we going to run out of money?

